      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the measure of \\(\\angle CED\\) given that \\(\\angle BAE = 43^\\circ\\) and \\(AB\\) is a diameter of the circle centered at \\(O\\).\n\nFirst, observe that since \\(AB\\) is a diameter, the angle \\(\\angle AEB\\) is a right angle (\\(\\angle AEB = 90^\\circ\\)) because any angle subtended by a diameter in a circle is a right angle.\n\nNext, let's consider the tangents at points \\(B\\) and \\(E\\). The tangent at \\(B\\) is perpendicular to the radius \\(OB\\), and the tangent at \\(E\\) is perpendicular to the radius \\(OE\\). Therefore, \\(\\angle OEB = 90^\\circ\\) and \\(\\angle OEA = 90^\\circ\\).\n\nSince \\(C\\) is the intersection of the tangent at \\(B\\) and the line \\(AE\\), and \\(D\\) is the intersection of the tangent at \\(E\\) and the line \\(AE\\), we can use the fact that the angle between a tangent and a chord through the point of contact is equal to the angle in the alternate segment.\n\nThus, \\(\\angle OBC = \\angle ABC\\) because they are angles in the alternate segment to \\(\\angle OAB\\). Similarly, \\(\\angle OED = \\angle ECD\\) because they are angles in the alternate segment to \\(\\angle OCA\\).\n\nNow, consider \\(\\triangle OBC\\) and \\(\\triangle OED\\). Since \\(BO = OE\\) (both are radii of the circle), and \\(\\angle OBC = \\angle OED\\) (by the alternate segment theorem), we have \\(\\triangle OBC \\cong \\triangle OED\\) by the AA (Angle-Angle) similarity criterion.\n\nThis implies that \\(\\angle BOC = \\angle EOD\\). Since \\(\\angle BAE = 43^\\circ\\) and \\(\\angle AEB = 90^\\circ\\), we have \\(\\angle BOE = 90^\\circ\\). Therefore, \\(\\angle OBE = \\angle OEB - \\angle OBE = 90^\\circ - 43^\\circ = 47^\\circ\\).\n\nSince \\(\\angle OBE = \\angle BOC\\) and \\(\\angle EOD = \\angle OED\\), we have \\(\\angle CED = \\angle OED = \\angle OBE = 47^\\circ\\).\n\nThus, the measure of \\(\\angle CED\\) is \\(\\boxed{47}\\).",
